A||N||22||mindscape||1988 GT 2.8 to 3.1||||1||
Z||000000||mindscape||07-05-2001||12:04 PM||||What issues should be taken into account when converting a 2.8 to a 3.1 engine?  I suppose there is more to it than just swapping out the crank shaft and connecting rods.||||reg||1
Z||000001||85blackgt||07-05-2001||01:04 PM||||i was gonna do the convertion but i got a deal on another fiero so im sellin my 90k 3.1 for $300 cnd!<P>------------------<BR>I stick my head out the window,when its closed instead of air i get glass stuck into my head,the city's sounding i can't seem to stop the pounding, can't keep my thoughts from flying round, not sure  what im thinking about, i got SOUL DOUBT!!!- NOFX<P>1986 Fiero SE 2.8lv6||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steve Normington||07-05-2001||01:14 PM||||For an 88 2.8, there is no changes needed to convert it to a 3.1 other than crank, rods, and pistons. You might go to an adjustable fuel pressure regulator to help feed the 3.1, but it is not required.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mindscape||07-05-2001||05:30 PM||||I suppose a list is in order to clarify the details of doing a conversion.<P>1.  Use 3.1 crank shaft.<BR>2.  Use 3.1 connecting rods.<BR>3.  Use 2.8 pistons?<BR>    The 3.1 pistions are dished for valve clearance reasons.  I do not want to drop  the compression.<BR>4.  I plan to use an overdrive transmission.<BR>    Is there a flex plate issue?<BR>5.  Harmonic balancer issue.  Will the stock 2.8 pulley work?<P>According to another source the 3.1 crank has to be balanced for the 2.8?  Comments?||||reg||1
Z||000004||mindscape||07-05-2001||05:32 PM||||I suppose a list is in order to clarify the details of doing a conversion.<P>1.  Use 3.1 crank shaft.<BR>2.  Use 3.1 connecting rods.<BR>3.  Use 2.8 pistons?<BR>    The 3.1 pistions are dished for valve clearance reasons.  I do not want to drop  the compression.<BR>4.  I plan to use an overdrive transmission.<BR>    Is there a flex plate issue?<BR>5.  Harmonic balancer issue.  Will the stock 2.8 pulley work with the 3.1 balancer?<P>According to another source the 3.1 crank has to be balanced for the 2.8?  Comments?<P>I thought the 3.1 cranks were internally balanced?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steve Normington||07-05-2001||06:08 PM||||<B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>The 3.1 pistions are dished for valve clearance reasons. I do not want to drop the compression.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think you'd have to use the 3.1 pistons. Remember that a 3.1 and 2.8 have the exact same block/heads. If the 3.1 needs dished pistons for valve clearance, than a 2.8 would too.<P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Is there a flex plate issue?<BR>5. Harmonic balancer issue. Will the stock 2.8 pulley work with the 3.1 balancer?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Since the 88 2.8 was internally ballanced, the 88 2.8 flexplate/flywheel and the 88 2.8 harmonic balancer will work with the 3.1 without any balancing. Whether this will work with the OD AT depends on the transmission. Note: you might want to get a new harmonic balancer as they can get "set" to a certain crank.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||07-05-2001||06:32 PM||||Nope......there are two variations to the 3.1 (as is with the 2.8).  You have those with aluminum heads and those with cast iron heads.  The 2.8/3.1's with aluminum heads are the ones with dished pistons......you must replace these when installing the engine in a Fiero (with the Fieros iron heads).<P>There is a 3.1 with iron heads that would require no mods.....it came on the 90's minivans......Transport, etc.  If it has iron heads then the shortblock is a bolt on for the Fiero (no need to replace pistons).<P>The heads can even be used, but I think they have the smaller valves, so it would be better to use the Fieros heads.<P>These are neutrally balanced, so you'll need a neutrally balanced flywheel/flexplate (like on the '88).<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||07-05-2001||07:33 PM||||To convert an 88 2.8 to a 3.1, you only need to change the crank and pistons(wrist pin holes are in a different location) and maybe up the fuel pressure a tad. The rods are the same in both engines and the pistons should be for the iron head version. The 88 flywheel or flex plate is already neutrally balanced.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dave Gunsul||07-05-2001||08:49 PM||||You can get a 3.1 stroker kit and use your original block.<BR>The rods are the same between the 2.8 and the 3.1. The pistons and crank are different.<BR>All V6 Fiero balancers are at zero balance so any will do.<P>As stated above: the alum headed version is the one with the dished pistons. <BR>The 88 is already internally balanced so the <BR>flywheel/flexplate is not an issue.<P>Pisa sells a 3.1 stroker kit.<BR>check it out at: <A HREF="http://www.cybercars.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cybercars.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||4
Z||000009||mindscape||07-05-2001||11:57 PM||||Okay, let's get one thing straight.  I'm using the 2.8 cast iron heads.  I do not want to use the 3.1 dished pistons because this will increase the combustion chamber size -&gt; resulting in a drop in compression.<P>Will the 2.8 flat head pistons work?  I suppose I will find out when its on the stand next week.  I understand that as long as the pistons do not come out the of block that they will work fine.  However, if the wrist pin holes are in a different location, then I will have to acquire a set of flat head pistons for this project.<P>I just thouht that it was odd that the ARI and PISA stroker kits come with dished pistons.<P>A fellow I know has a junkyard.  The plan is to get a crankshaft and pistons.  I suppose we could get into the pros and cons of reusing pistons and reconditioned cranks.  Suggestions are welcomed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Any advantages to using molly rings over standard?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Steve Normington||07-06-2001||12:50 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B>Nope......there are two variations to the 3.1 (as is with the 2.8).  You have those with aluminum heads and those with cast iron heads. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey, new fact today. Sorry about the wrong info, I was thinking about the stroker kit.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Greg Piet||07-06-2001||01:14 PM||||Another good reason to use <B>dished</B> pistons is the following (assuming my logic is correct)...<BR>A stroker kit will increase the travel that the piston goes from bottom to top.<BR>If the stock piston goes to an even higher position than before with a new crank(thus a stroker), the volume will be compressed even more (the maximum volume in the cylinder is bigger and now the minimum volume is smaller).  Either of these items will increase the compression, and both factors will increase it even more.  With this higher compression your head must be in cherry shape, and you will have problems running a blower in the future likely.<P>I like to not think too much when doing automotive repairs as it is my distraction from the real world so I go by this theory...<BR>If a racing engine company (ARI) and PISA use dished pistons, I will bet they have a pretty darn good reason.<P>Also the thought of a cam with a higher lobe, and different rocker arms makes me think that the valves will open up even more which brings them closer to the piston(Fiero lifters may remedy this)...  I do not want my valves to slap my pistons.||||reg||1
Z||000012||mindscape||07-06-2001||10:03 PM||||I understand the reasons why dished and recessed pistons are used.  However, on the 1988 2.8 that is currently apart, the flat top pistons do not come up and out of the block.  So, no clearance problem.  I can't afford to buy a kit from ARI or PISA, so I'm going at it myself.  According to the part books Federal Mogul/Sealed Power and Badger 3.1 pistons for use with cast iron heads are rated at 8.8:1 compression ratio.  These pistons have a .105" recess.  Thinking about it logically I do not believe that the pistons jut out the top of a block on a factory 3.1 engine.  So, why would they on a 3.1 conversion?<P>I am going to use a melling MTC-5 performance cam shaft, which isn't an agressive performance cam.  This cam is specified for use with 2.8 and 3.1 engines.  I will verify that clearance isn't a problem.  This cam is suppose to improve low to mid range performance.  My reasons for the cam shaft and 3.1 conversion are related to the fact that I'm also converting to a overdrive transaxle.  Thus, improved low end performace is good.<P>When I posted the engine question I was looking for a simple answer.  The 1988 2.8 is easily converted by replacing the crankshaft and pistons; connecting rods are the same. Because . . .<P>Now to beat the overdrive transaxle issue to death.<P>A thank you to all!||||reg||1
Z||000013||88formula||07-06-2001||10:27 PM||||Give me those camshaft specifications and I will give you some dyno2000 predictions on what kind of power it will make. <P>Also about the compression ratio go here. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008285.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008285.html</A> <P>Watch out with the overdrive transmission They won't necessarily give you better performance. In fact with a lower stall converter you could end up with worse performance.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||mindscape||07-07-2001||03:08 AM||||Camshaft specifications for Melling MTC-5<P>I'm using 1.5 rockers so...<BR>                           Gross<BR>                           Valve Center<BR>    Opens  Closes Dur Lift Lift  Line<BR>INT  5 ATC 29 ABC 204 .280 .420  107<BR>EXT 44 BBC 10 BTC 214 .295 .443  117||||reg||1
Z||000015||mindscape||07-31-2001||02:41 AM||||Are there any Fiero ECM software issues when going from a 2.8 to a 3.1(3.2) conversion?||||reg||5
Z||000016||mrfiero||07-31-2001||03:11 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mindscape:<BR><B>Are there any Fiero ECM software issues when going from a 2.8 to a 3.1(3.2) conversion?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Not unless you are transplanting an aluminum head version.  If you build up an iron head 3.1, then it is essentially the same as a 2.8 and the Fiero ECM will do just fine.  <P>Even with the popular 3.4 upgrade the stock Fiero 2.8 ECM is used.<P>If you want the "benefits" of the aluminum head 3.1 or 3.4 then you will need to use the ECM for that particular engine (since lots of wiring mods are required, etc.).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||gt boy||07-31-2001||10:58 PM||||what is the benifit from doing the 3.1 conversion...i am thinking of doing it also but adding a cam and roller rockers. is it that much better than the stock 2.8 with cam and roller rockers....and what about the smaller pulley...does it make much of a difference?||||reg||8
Z||000018||Shaun41178||07-31-2001||11:20 PM||||Ok Gm used dished pistons in their engine.  Now as I understand it its because the stroke was increased and because of that if you use 2.8 flat top pistons the valves will hit the pistons when opened.  But if you say the 2.8 pistons don't stick out higher then the deck of the block then maybe they won't.  do a dry run and see if they hit.  If not then you are in the clear<P>If not then calculate your compression ratio.  it will probably be pretty high<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds||||reg||1
Z||000019||mindscape||08-01-2001||05:22 AM||||I recently finished my 2.8 to 3.1 rebuild.  Will the pistons hit the valves?  I believe the answer is, NO, because the pistons do not stick out any higher than the deck of the block.  However, I'm not using flat top pistons because I could not get my hands on an affordable set.<P>Thus far I've driven the car 20 miles, and  can tell there is a definite personality change; now to make it through the break-in period.||||reg||1
Z||000020||GT GT 87||08-02-2001||12:28 AM||||When you stoke an engine you are automatically raising the c.r. I am building a 3.2.(slight overbore) You have to use dished pistons , it has nothing to do with the valves hitting the pistons!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000021||mindscape||08-02-2001||02:37 AM||||I will agree with that... believe that it has nothing to do with the valves hitting the pistons.  BUT, the fellow at PISA who wrote me about their kit said it was why the kit included dished pistons.  Needless to say, I didn't buy a kit.  I used a 981 crank from Crank Rebuilders that was turned .25mm.  I'll talk it over with the fellows who talked me though the rebuild.  According to the Federal book the pistons in this application result in approximately 8.8:1 ratio.||||reg||1
Z||000022||saleenfiero||08-02-2001||02:13 PM||||so, what kind of performance improvement are we looking at here???||||reg||1
